            <ArticleTitle>Granulomatous diseases: Kids are not just little people.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>555-565</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0738-081X(17)30127-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clindermatol.2017.08.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Granulomatous diseases represent a heterogeneous group of conditions characterized by histiocytic inflammation that affect patients of any age. These diseases differ widely in their pathogenesis and include infectious and noninfectious conditions. This review focuses on noninfectious granulomatous conditions, with particular emphasis on age-related differences in the onset, epidemiology, clinical manifestations, prognosis, and age-specific management of specific granulomatous disorders. Knowledge of age-specific aspects of granulomatous conditions in adults and children improves both the extent of the diagnostic workup and the management of these patients.</AbstractText>
